{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs is set to end, and a new law will re-criminalize possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option includes participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines, and personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to curb public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain locations. Supporters argue that it allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources.\n\n*** \nHowever, opponents worry that this may create an inconsistent and unfair policy landscape across the state. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to establish treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the justice system towards addiction and mental health services.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, it's worth considering the broader implications for the country, where people of all age groups struggle with addiction. Some may have to bite their tongue when faced with the harsh realities of the new law. Meanwhile, others may be forced to eat into their savings to access treatment. For those struggling with addiction, shopping for a solution can be a daunting task. \nP.P.S As the country grapples with the complexities of addiction, it's clear that a one-size-fits-all approach won't work.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.4740769863128662}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0026958573143929243}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -1.3